  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Demonstration centers to facilitate Dutch horticultural solutions adoption by Beninese farmers and entrepreneurs.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Benin and the Kingdom of the Netherlands have agreed to strengthen their trade relations. This follows several years of successful cooperation in the sectors of water, food security, and health. These partnerships have helped improve the business climate, promote inclusion, and enhance social well-being.
 
The Beninese government has introduced reforms and created new tools to attract investments. These efforts open opportunities for sustainable investment projects in key Dutch expertise areas such as agriculture and water management.

After the fourth trade mission on sustainable agriculture and water, a follow-up initiative was launched. It aims to build on the opportunities identified and to maintain collaboration between Dutch and Beninese partners.
 
The Demonstration Program for Horticultural and Potato Solutions will be implemented through two demonstration centers, one in the north and one in the south of Benin. The program will last three years. It promotes sustainable and efficient farming practices and helps Beninese farmers increase productivity. It also creates a platform for Dutch companies to access the Beninese market. The project will start with an initial group of 10 Dutch companies, and more companies are expected to join in the following years. The initiative remains open to any Dutch agricultural company wishing to benefit from the project but can also contribute meaningful value through relevant technologies or expertise that can strengthen the local horticulture and potato sectors. 
 
The project is led by Holland Green Tech, a Dutch company that has been in Benin for almost six years. The company understands the local market and connects Dutch horticultural technologies with the needs of Beninese farmers and cooperatives.

Building on successful models from Ghana, Rwanda, Tanzania, and Senegal, the centers will serve as places to showcase technology and business solutions. They will speed up the use of climate-smart farming technologies, create jobs for youth and women, and attract Dutch investments in Benin&#x2019;s agribusiness sector.</narrative>
  </description>
